---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';

const rows = [
  ['Sessions survive closing the terminal', 'Yes', 'Yes'],
  ['Sessions survive a dropped SSH connection', 'Yes, and the tab reconnects by itself', 'Yes, after you ssh in and reattach'],
  ['Where you see sessions', 'Native tabs and a sidebar in a macOS app', 'Windows and panes inside any terminal'],
  ['Scrollback', 'The terminal’s own: scroll, select and search with the mouse; 16 MB per session, more on disk', 'tmux’s copy mode; the outer terminal can’t scroll it'],
  ['Keys', 'macOS shortcuts, no prefix key', 'A prefix key (Ctrl-b) before each command'],
  ['Remote setup', 'Installs and updates its daemon on the host for you', 'Install tmux on each host yourself'],
  ['Updates', 'Running sessions move to the new version without restarting', 'Running sessions keep the old server until it exits'],
  ['Command line', 'swarmctl: ls, new, attach, dump and more, locally or with --host', 'tmux’s own commands, very scriptable'],
  ['Platforms', 'App on macOS 13+; hosts on Linux or macOS', 'Almost any Unix, in any terminal'],
  ['Price and license', 'Free; app AGPL-3.0, daemon MIT or Apache-2.0', 'Free; ISC license'],
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is Swarm a replacement for tmux?',
    a: 'For keeping work running when you close the terminal or lose the connection, yes: each Swarm tab is a session owned by a background daemon, on your Mac or on a server. tmux is still the better fit if you need sessions on a machine you only reach from Linux or Windows, or want one tool in every terminal.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use tmux inside Swarm?',
    a: 'Yes. A Swarm session runs your normal shell, so tmux, screen and full-screen programs work in it as in any terminal.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to install anything on my servers?',
    a: 'No. When you connect to a host, Swarm offers to install its daemon, swarmd, in your home folder; it needs no root. Static Linux builds are also available to set servers up yourself.',
  },
];

const faqPage = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map(({ q, a }) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a } })),
};
---

<Guide
  kind="# compare"
  title="Swarm vs tmux: persistent terminal sessions on macOS"
  description="Swarm and tmux both keep terminal sessions running when you close the window or lose SSH. How they differ: native tabs and scrollback, remote setup, keys, updates and platforms."
  jsonLd={[faqPage]}
>
  <h1>Swarm vs tmux</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Both keep your shells running when you close the window or the SSH connection drops. tmux does it with a
    multiplexer inside your terminal. Swarm does it with a daemon that owns each session, and shows the sessions as
    native macOS tabs.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pick tmux</strong> if you work across Linux, macOS and Windows terminals, already live in its key bindings, or need a session that several people attach to on a shared server.</li>
    <li><strong>Pick Swarm</strong> if you're on a Mac and want persistent local and SSH sessions without a prefix key, with the terminal's own scrollback, mouse selection and search, and with remote hosts set up for you.</li>
    <li><strong>Or use both:</strong> tmux runs fine inside a Swarm session.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Side by side</h2>
  <div class="table">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th></th><th>Swarm</th><th>tmux</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map(([what, swarm, tmux]) => (
          <tr><td><strong>{what}</strong></td><td>{swarm}</td><td>{tmux}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How they keep sessions alive</h2>
  <p>
    tmux runs a server process that owns your shells, and your terminal shows a tmux client drawing them. Close the
    terminal and the server keeps going; open a terminal and run <code>tmux attach</code> to see it again. On a remote
    machine you ssh in first, then attach.
  </p>
  <p>
    Swarm splits the same job differently. A small daemon, <code>swarmd</code>, owns every session, on your Mac or on a
    server over SSH. The Swarm app is only a view: quitting it, updating it or closing a tab leaves the sessions
    running, and reopening shows each one with its earlier output. For remote hosts the app connects with your own ssh
    config and keys, and reconnects by itself after sleep or a network change.
  </p>

  <h2>Day to day</h2>
  <h3>Scrollback and search</h3>
  <p>
    Inside tmux your terminal sees only the visible screen, so scrolling and searching history means tmux's copy mode.
    In Swarm each tab is a normal terminal view: scroll with the trackpad, select with the mouse, and search the
    output. Commands show as blocks with their exit codes and durations, thanks to the Warp engine Swarm is built on.
  </p>
  <h3>Splits and layout</h3>
  <p>
    tmux has windows and panes driven by keys. Swarm has tabs, sidebar groups and split panes: right-click a session for{' '}
    <strong>Open in Split</strong>, or drag it onto a pane, to see local and remote sessions side by side.
  </p>
  <h3>Scripting</h3>
  <p>
    tmux is very scriptable. Swarm's <code>swarmctl</code> covers the common jobs: list, create, attach to, rename, dump
    and end sessions, on this Mac or on any host with <code>--host</code>.
  </p>
  <pre class="term"><span class="p">$</span> swarmctl ls --host build-box
<span class="p">$</span> swarmctl attach release --host build-box   <span class="d"># ctrl-\ detaches</span>
<span class="p">$</span> swarmctl dump release &gt; build.log</pre>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  {faq.map(({ q, a }) => (
    <>
      <h3>{q}</h3>
      <p>{a}</p>
    </>
  ))}
</Guide>
